Okay, here's a JavaScript script that demonstrates static initialization blocks and compares them to constructors. This script aims to clarify the concepts explained on the MDN page:


```javascript
// Example using static initialization blocks
class MyClassWithStaticBlock {
  static message = "Hello from static block!";

  static {
    console.log("Static initialization block executing...");
    // Perform one-time setup here...  For example, loading external data or performing complex calculations
    this.message = "Static block updated the message!"; // You can modify static members
  }

  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    console.log(`Constructor for ${this.name} executing.`);
  }

  static greet() {
    console.log(this.message); // Access static member
  }
}

// Example using only the constructor (no static block)

class MyClassWithoutStaticBlock {
  static message = "Hello from the constructor!";
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
    console.log(`Constructor for ${this.name} executing.`);
  }
    static greet() {
    console.log(this.message);
  }
}

// Usage examples:
console.log("MyClassWithStaticBlock:");
MyClassWithStaticBlock.greet(); // Access static method
new MyClassWithStaticBlock("Alice"); //Create object
new MyClassWithStaticBlock("Bob"); //Create object


console.log("\nMyClassWithoutStaticBlock:");
MyClassWithoutStaticBlock.greet();
new MyClassWithoutStaticBlock("Charlie");
new MyClassWithoutStaticBlock("David");

//Static block only runs once for the class, not for each object creation. Constructor runs for each new object
```

This script showcases two classes: one using a static initialization block and another using only a constructor. The output in the console will clearly illustrate the difference in execution timing and demonstrate how static properties are initialized and accessed.  The comments within the script highlight the key differences and explain the purpose of the static block.  Running this script will make the behavior of static initialization blocks clear.
